  "summary": "A distinctive vernacular architecture characterized by beehive-shaped mud huts with geometric patterns and conical roofs.",
  "description": "These structures feature tall, beehive-shaped domes made exclusively from mud without wooden supports, with distinctive geometric patterns etched into the walls. They represent an adaptation to the local environment and the cultural preferences of the Musgum people, showcasing sophisticated earth-building techniques.",
  "culturalContext": "Musgum earth architecture — Cameroon and Chad's beehive shell huts (teleuk) — solve climate with pure geometry: clay-and-grass domes with ribbed surface ridges (drainage+structure+climbing scaffold), tiny crowned ventilation, and compound clusters of paired domes around grain courts. The ridged profile (musgum \"herringbone\") has been cited by Gaudí scholars and gridshell engineers alike; the huts build in days and last decades, renewed by women masons in family transmission.",
